WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.560
A target makes nerve growth factor, called NGF, but too little for every sympathetic

2
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:10.960
neuron that reaches it.

3
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.120
So which neurons get to live?

4
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.280
Share it evenly, and they all live or all die together.

5
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.540
If the first axon took everything, nothing would explain how a neuron receiving some

6
00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.320
NGF could fall behind.

7
00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.500
Something else must pick the winners.

8
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.540
In mouse sympathetic neurons, NGF raises its own receptor, TrkA.

9
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.660
With NGF, TrkA rises, and without it, TrkA falls.

10
00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.420
So a neuron with a little more TrkA collects more NGF.

11
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.160
It makes still more TrkA and pulls further ahead.

12
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.680
That loop is positive feedback, but the model also needed longer NGF signals.

13
00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:59.733
In simulations with steady NGF production, feedback on both TrkA level and signal

14
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:02.573
duration split the neurons into winners and losers.

15
00:01:03.353 --> 00:01:06.873
A young neuron is naive and responds poorly to NGF.

16
00:01:07.473 --> 00:01:11.373
Enough NGF carries it over a threshold, which is sensitization.

17
00:01:11.713 --> 00:01:15.253
It raises TrkA, and that is a competitive advantage.

18
00:01:15.253 --> 00:01:19.853
Winning the supply is selection, and neighbours that stayed behind lose.

19
00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:22.473
Winners do more than pull ahead.

20
00:01:23.233 --> 00:01:26.793
Strongly supported neurons make B, D, N, F, and N, T, 4.

21
00:01:27.413 --> 00:01:30.793
These act through p75 on weakly supported neighbours.

22
00:01:31.553 --> 00:01:34.353
In this setting, p75 can promote death.

23
00:01:35.073 --> 00:01:41.253
A neuron rich in TrkA is protected, and a neuron poor in TrkA is not.

24
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:43.277
Does punishment matter?

25
00:01:43.697 --> 00:01:49.017
In simulations, it made the split faster, because weak neurons lingered without it.

26
00:01:49.557 --> 00:01:53.857
In mice lacking p75, the loss is delayed but not prevented.

27
00:01:54.477 --> 00:01:59.357
In the lab's own study, neuron number falls more slowly and reaches the control

28
00:01:59.357 --> 00:02:03.177
level. The losers still die, because they lack enough NGF.

29
00:02:04.117 --> 00:02:07.277
So a limited supply does not pick the winners alone.

30
00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:11.837
A small early lead boosts TrkA and signal length.

31
00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:17.137
Winners punish weak neighbours through p75, and the split comes faster.

32
00:02:17.757 --> 00:02:22.697
All this comes from one cell type, the sympathetic neuron, in mice and rat cultures.

33
00:02:23.437 --> 00:02:28.517
Remember the rule: feedback turns a small lead into survival, and punishment speeds

34
00:02:28.517 --> 00:02:28.957
it up.
